--TEST--
`Core\Html::escape` answers a carrier, so the eager-escape habit needs `toSource` written at the site and produces the same document either way — ADR 0133 §§ 1, 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0133 § 1's predicate, seen from the program rather than from the
// registry: the HTML sink escapes on its own and escaping is not idempotent,
// so an escaped value that were a plain `string` would be escaped a second
// time by a sink that could not tell it from text. The carrier is what makes
// the second application impossible to write by accident.
tainted string $name = "Tom & Jerry";
Core\Html\Markup $safe = Core\Html::escape($name);
echo $safe, "\n";

// § 2's *Context* program, said explicitly: a lifted literal composes with an
// escaped value, and the composition is the carrier again. Neither half is
// escaped by the `+`, because both already passed whichever rule made them
// `Markup`.
echo ("Hello " as Core\Html\Markup) + $safe, "\n";

// The non-idempotence, demonstrated rather than asserted: taking the bytes
// back out and escaping them again is a thing a program can still do, and it
// produces the `&amp;amp;` that the carrier exists to stop happening by
// accident. It takes a written reason to say it now, which is the difference.
Core\Html\Markup $twice = Core\Html::escape(
    Core\Html::toSource($safe, "escaping an already-escaped fragment on purpose")
);
echo $twice, "\n";

// Three applications, so the growth is shown to compound rather than being one
// surprising line. A sink that auto-escaped a `string` answer would produce
// this on the second `echo` of the first value, with nothing written at the
// site to say so.
echo Core\Html::escape(Core\Html::toSource($twice, "and once more, to show it compounds")), "\n";

// The carrier is not text as far as the type system is concerned, which is the
// whole guarantee: the only way to a `string` is by name, and it is greppable.
string $source = Core\Html::toSource($safe, "storing the rendered fragment in a column");
echo $source, "\n";
--EXPECT--
Tom &amp; Jerry
Hello Tom &amp; Jerry
Tom &amp;amp; Jerry
Tom &amp;amp;amp; Jerry
Tom &amp; Jerry
